    "body": "Great on any Friday: Biolite LED Site Lights\n1968 Chevrolet Chevelle 427 Is Rarer Than a COPO, Packs Dual-Quad Setup\n11 photos\nPhoto: Geoff Stunkard's Zookeeper's Diary/YouTube\nIntroduced for the 1964 model year, the Chevelle was Chevrolet's first mid-size car. Aimed primarily at the Ford Fairlane , the Chevelle became part of the muscle car wars almost as soon as it hit dealerships.\nThe SS version came with a variety of high-performance engines, but Chevrolet also offered the limited-edition Z-16, powered by a 375-horsepower big-block V8. In 1970, the Chevelle gained the notorious 454-cubic-inch (7.4-liter) LS6. Rated at a whopping 450 horsepower, it turned the Chevelle into America's most potent production car.\nHowever, some powerful Chevelles were also born in private shops. Although mostly known for Camaro-based muscle cars, Yenko also built a limited-series Chevelle using the COPO ordering system. These cars were fitted with 427-cubic-inch (7.0-liter) V8 engines at a time when mid-sized Chevrolets were available with mills larger than 400 cubic inches (6.6 liters).\nThe COPO Chevelle is even rarer than the COPO 427 Camaro . The latter was built in about 1,000 units, 198 of which were ordered by Don Yenko. The COPO Chevelle, on the other hand, was produced in just 99 examples. It's also far scarcer than the Z-16 and SS 454 LS6, but it's by no means the rarest high-performance Chevelle out there.\nThat prize goes to a lesser known 427-equipped model that was conceived by Dick Harrell. A notable drag racer, Mr. Harrell is also responsible for the COPO ZL1 Camaro, which he conceived for the Fred Gibb Chevrolet dealership. A year before that, however, he dropped 427-cubic-inch V8s in the 1968 Chevelle.\nMuch like Yenko, Dick used the L72, but he also built a few cars with the Corvette's L88 mill. Most of these cars came with 450 horsepower on tap with a four-barrel carburetor or 500 horsepower when fitted with a dual-quad setup. How many of these Chevelles were built? Well, Chevelle experts estimate that 12 units left the Dick Harrell Performance Center in Kansas City, but only five have been documented so far.\nIf you haven't seen one yet, a pristine example was recently on display at the 2026 Muscle Car and Corvette Nationals (MCACN) . And the story goes that it's the only Harrell-built street car out there. It's in perfect condition inside and out and has only 11,342 miles (18,253 km) on the odometer.\nThe Chevelle was filmed while being inspected by MCACN's Vintage Certification team. The program recognizes unrestored original classics by inspecting them for approximately three hours, including a detailed examination underneath the car on a lift. The certification team then provides each owner with a detailed listing of their findings in a comprehensive report.\nThere's no info on what this inspection unearthed, but given the low mileage, it's safe to say that this Chevelle is highly original and perhaps the finest surviving Harrell car. It's also likely worth well over a quarter million dollars. Hit the play button below to check it out.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Chevelle 1968 Chevrolet Chevelle 427 V8 Chevrolet classic car muscle car Dick Harrell mcacn 2026\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT 5 Most Iconic Production Cars Finished in an Eye-Catching Shade of Orange",
